# Both uses of a polymorphic parameter reaching the result type.
#
# `use_twice` calls `f` at Int and at Bool, and keeps *both* answers --
# so the two instantiations are not merely made and discarded, they are
# what the result is built out of. That is the difference from
# tests/typecheck_type_first_class.pyk, where the two uses are joined
# into one String, and from tests/typecheck_hm_annotated_rank2.pyk,
# where they are printed for effect and the function is worth unit.
#
# `id` is written with no annotation at all, so it is *inferred*
# polymorphic and then passed where a written `all` demands a scheme.
# An inferred scheme and a written one are the same kind of thing here,
# which is what makes that work (see docs/type_inference.md, "Rank one,
# and why that is the whole story").
#
# The signature also spells the inner function type without parentheses.
# `a -> a` and `(a) -> a` are one type (PYKING.md, "Type variables"), so
# the printed AST below normalises it to the parenthesized form.

def use_twice : (all a. a -> a) -> (Int, Bool)
def use_twice(f):
    return (f(42), f(true))


def id(x):
    return x


print(use_twice(id))
